(c) For the purpose of this Act, "passenger information" shall be deemed to include the following information.

(1) the person's full name and date of birth;

(2) the country to which the person belongs;

(3) the information in the person's valid travel document;

(4) the vessel information (for example, the flight number and seat number).

(d) For the purpose of this Act, "crew information" shall be deemed to include the following information.

(1) the person's full name and date of birth;

(2) the country to which the person belongs;

(3) the information in the person's valid travel document or the person's crew identification information;

(4) information on the person's position and duty.

(e) Information such as that specified in subsections (c) and (d) of this section shall not include the

biometric information of the passenger or crew member to whom that information relates.

(f) Crew and passenger information shared with immigration under this section shall be collected and processed by immigration in accordance with the Data Protection Act, solely for the purpose of granting immigration clearance to those persons and maintaining border security under this Act.

(g) Crew and passenger information shared with immigration under this section shall be stored for a period of at most 48 (forty-eight) hours from the time that information is received by immigration. And the circumstances in which that period may be extended, and the procedure to be followed in deleting that information upon expiry of that period, shall be specified in the regulation made under this Act.

(h) If a law enforcement agency in the Maldives or abroad requests in writing to obtain crew and passenger information shared with immigration under this section, immigration shall give access to that information or share that information solely for the following purposes.

(1) verifying the identity of the person and granting immigration clearance;

(2) law enforcement and maintaining border security;

(i) If the owner of a vessel or the party operating the vessel does not share the information specified in subsection (a) of this section with immigration in accordance with the regulation made under this Act, that party commits an offence. And, measures may be taken against a party acting in that manner in the manner specified in the regulation made under this Act.

Electronic Travel Authorisation
33. (a) In addition to the permit to stay in the Maldives specified in Chapter Five of this Act, immigration may establish arrangements [unclear] requiring citizens of countries determined in the regulation made under this Act to obtain, before travelling to the Maldives, permission to travel to the Maldives through an electronic system.

(b) The countries specified in subsection (a) of this section shall be determined by the Minister, on the advice of the Minister responsible for implementing policies related to foreign affairs.

(c) The regulation formulated in relation to the electronic system specified in subsection (b) of this section shall include the following matters.

(1) the manner of applying for a permit under that system;

(2) the principles on which permits will be issued under that system;

(3) the circumstances in which a permit may not be issued under that system;

(4) the validity period of a permit issued under that system, and the number of times the Maldives may be entered within that period;

Passport regulation

44. The Controller General shall make and enforce a passport regulation under this Act, including the following matters:

(a) the principles for issuing passports of different categories and types (including the period of issue and the information to be included in them);

(b) the procedures relating to issuing and renewing passports;

(c) the manner in which biometric information for making the passport shall be collected and stored;

(d) the fees payable for making a passport;

(e) the procedure for temporarily withholding passports;

(f) the procedure relating to issuing temporary travel documents;

(g) the procedure for cancelling a passport in a circumstance specified in Article 41 or Article 42 of this Act.

CHAPTER FIVE
Permission to Enter and Stay in the Maldives

Requirement of permission to enter the Maldives

45. (a) For a foreigner to enter the Maldives and to stay in the Maldives, there must be permission to enter the Maldives.

(b) For the purpose of subsection (a) of this Article, permission to enter the Maldives means holding a visa issued under this Chapter or such permission on arrival.

Persons who may not be granted permission to enter the Maldives

46. The following categories of foreigners are persons who may not be granted permission to enter the Maldives:

(a) persons considered likely to pose a danger to the sovereignty or national security of the Maldives;

(b) persons belonging to terrorist groups, and persons considered to have links with such groups, and persons who have provided information to terrorists;

(c) persons who have committed, or are suspected of having committed, an offence determined to be a criminal offence under international law;

(d) persons against whom the International Criminal Court has brought charges or issued an arrest warrant, or who have been listed by the International Criminal Police Organization;

(e) persons who have fled from the justice system or an investigative authority of another country;

(f) persons proven [unclear] to have a disease dangerous to public health, or persons coming to the Maldives from a country or region determined by the ministry responsible for health or an international health authority to be one where a dangerous disease is spreading;

(g) persons for whom deportation from the Maldives has been [unclear], or for whom deportation has been determined, or temporarily

persons belonging to countries determined by the Government of the Maldives from among the countries with which relations have been severed;

(h) persons deported under this Act in such a manner that they may not come to the Maldives;

(i) persons who, on [unclear] grounds, do, or are considered to be attempting to do, something that would harm the social or religious unity of the Maldives;

(j) holders of Israeli passports;

(k) persons determined in another law as persons who may not be granted permission to enter the Maldives.

Issuing visas

47. (a) Visas for citizens of foreign countries to enter the Maldives shall be issued by Maldives Immigration. And a visa so issued may grant permission to enter the Maldives within a specified period, and, in the event of entering the Maldives within that period, to stay in the Maldives for a specified period.

(b) When granting a foreigner permission to stay in the Maldives through a visa issued by Maldives Immigration under this Article, the Controller General has the discretion to grant permission to enter the Maldives more than once under that visa.

